{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"6b6c1cf2-5533-494a-8f29-9a25ef972107","title":"Chance a dart lands in a circle inside a square"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"6b6c1cf2-5533-494a-8f29-9a25ef972107","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Imagine a dart lands uniformly at random inside this square. The circle just touches all four sides. We want the chance of landing inside the circle.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"dartboard","region":"left","frame":{"x":[-1.5,1.5],"y":[-1.5,1.5],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"polyline","id":"square","points":[[-1,-1],[1,-1],[1,1],[-1,1]],"closed":true},{"kind":"circle","id":"circle","center":[0,0],"r":1}]},{"op":"text","id":"heading","region":"C1","content":"Probability from area"},{"op":"equation","id":"ratio","anchor":{"el":"heading","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"P=\\frac{\\text{area of circle}}{\\text{area of square}}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"6b6c1cf2-5533-494a-8f29-9a25ef972107","step":{"id":"s2","script":"If the radius is r, the square's side is twice r. So the circle's area is pi r squared, and the square's is four r squared.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"dimensions","on":"dartboard","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"center","at":[0,0]},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"center"},"to":[1,0],"label":"r"},{"kind":"measure","from":[-1,-1.2],"to":[1,-1.2],"label":"2r"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"areas","anchor":{"el":"ratio","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"A_{\\text{circle}}=\\pi r^2,\\qquad A_{\\text{square}}=(2r)^2=4r^2"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"6b6c1cf2-5533-494a-8f29-9a25ef972107","step":{"id":"s3","script":"The shaded circle is the successful region. Dividing areas cancels the radius, giving pi over four: about a seventy-nine percent chance.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"success","on":"dartboard","marks":[{"kind":"shade","inside":"circle"},{"kind":"text","at":[0,0.5],"text":"success"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"answer","anchor":{"el":"areas","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"P=\\frac{\\pi r^2}{4r^2}=\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\approx 0.785=78.5\\%"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Would doubling the circle's radius change this probability if it still touches all four sides?","expected_gist":"No; both areas grow by the same factor."}}}
{"type":"lesson.done","request_id":"6b6c1cf2-5533-494a-8f29-9a25ef972107","accepted_steps":3,"repairs":0,"dropped_steps":0,"sanitized_steps":0,"sanitized_fields":0}
